\title{FactorialSurvey: Design and Analysis of Factorial Survey Experiments}
\description{
\code{FactorialSurvey} supports the full workflow of a factorial survey
(vignette) experiment. A \emph{factorial survey} presents respondents with short
descriptions ("vignettes") whose attributes are experimentally varied, and
asks for a rating -- typically on a bounded scale such as 0 to 10. Because
the attributes are randomised by design, their effects on the rating can be
given a causal interpretation.

The package is organised around three stages.

\strong{1. Design.} \code{\link[=fs_defficient]{fs_defficient()}} selects a D-efficient subset of
vignettes from the full factorial and splits it into blocks;
\code{\link[=fs_design_eval]{fs_design_eval()}} reports level balance and attribute correlations;
\code{\link[=set_labels]{set_labels()}} attaches readable level labels to the resulting data frame.

\strong{2. Estimation.} \code{\link[=fs_betareg]{fs_betareg()}} fits a beta regression to bounded
ratings, mapping the observed scale onto the open unit interval and
returning average marginal effects on both the transformed and the original
scale.

\strong{3. Post-estimation.} \code{\link[=fs_mwtp]{fs_mwtp()}} converts a pair of coefficients
into a marginal willingness to pay, with a delta-method standard error and
confidence interval.
}
\section{A note on standard errors}{

In a typical factorial survey each respondent rates several vignettes, so
observations are clustered within respondents. Pass the respondent
identifier to \code{\link[=fs_betareg]{fs_betareg()}} through its \code{cluster} argument: standard
errors, marginal effects and willingness-to-pay intervals are then
cluster-robust. Without it, ratings are treated as independent and the
reported uncertainty is not valid; it is much too small for the effects of
respondent characteristics such as age or gender.
}

